NNAAMMEE
       pbmtoppa  -  convert  PBM  image to HP Printer Performance Architecture
       (PPA)


SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ppbbmmttooppppaa [_p_b_m___f_i_l_e [_p_p_a___f_i_l_e]]


DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppbbmmttooppppaa converts page images in PBM format to  Hewlett  Packard's  PPA
       (Printer  Performance  Architecture)  format,  which is the data stream
       format expected by some HP "Windows-only"  printers  including  the  HP
       Deskjet 820C series, the HP DeskJet 720 series, and the HP DeskJet 1000
       series.

       _p_b_m___f_i_l_e is the file specification of the input file or -- for  Standard
       Input.  The default is Standard Input.

       The  input  file contains one or more PBM images, with each one being a
       single page.  Each image must have the exact dimensions of a  page  (at
       600  pixels  per  inch in both directions).  Significantly, this is the
       format that Ghostscript produces.

       _p_p_a___f_i_l_e is the file specification of the output file or -- for Standard
       Output.  The default is Standard Output.

       To print Postscript on an HP PPA printer, just use Ghostscript with the
       ppbbmmrraaww (or ppbbmm) device driver.

       You can generate a test page for use with this program with ppbbmmppaaggee.

       You can also set up a printer filter so you can submit  PBM  input  di‐
       rectly  to  your  print  queue.   See  the documentation for your print
       spooler for information on how to do that, or look in  hp820install.doc
       for an example lpd print filter for Postscript and text files.

       Sometimes,  ppbbmmttooppppaa  generates a file which the printer will not print
       (because ppbbmmttooppppaa's input is  unprintable).   When  this  happens,  all
       three  lights blink to signal the error.  This is usually because there
       is material outside of the printer's printable area.  To make the  file
       print,  increase  the  margins  via ppbbmmttooppppaa options or a configuration
       file.  See the section on calibration ⟨#calibration⟩  below.


   AAbboouutt PPPPAA
       The PPA printer language is a far lower level language than most.  When
       you  use  a  PPA  printer,  most  of the processing that a conventional
       printer does is done instead on the computer end of the wire.  In  par‐
       ticular,  ppbbmmttooppppaa  has  to do "swath cutting," and "sweep formatting,"
       which other printers do themselves.  There is very little  intelligence
       inside  a  PPA  printer;  ppbbmmttooppppaa  generates  direct  controls for the
       printer's hardware.

       The design goal of PPA was to reduce the cost of a printer by  exploit‐
       ing  computing  resources already present in the computer that requests
       the printing.  CPU power, ROM, and RAM requirements inside the  printer
       are all reduced compared to a conventional printer.

       PPA was new in 1997.  It was preceded by Hewlett Packard's PCL (Printer
       Control Language) language.  HP manufactured PPA printers  for  only  a
       few years, and no one else ever did.


OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In  addition  to  the options common to all programs based on libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common Options ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ), ppbbmmttooppppaa  recognizes  the
       following command line options:



       --vv _v_e_r_s_i_o_n
              printer version (720, 820, or 1000)


       --xx _x_o_f_f
              horizontal offset adjustment in 1/600 inches.


       --yy _y_o_f_f
              vertical offset adjustment in 1/600 inches.


       --tt _t_o_p_m_a_r_g
              top margin in 1/600 inches    (default: 150 = 0.25 inch)


       --ll _l_e_f_t_m_a_r_g
              left margin in 1/600 inches   (default: 150 = 0.25 inch)


       --rr _r_i_g_h_t_m_a_r_g
              right margin in 1/600 inches (default: 150 = 0.25 inch)


       --bb _b_o_t_m_a_r_g
              bottom margin in 1/600 inches (default: 150 = 0.25 inch)


       --ss _p_a_p_e_r
              paper size: uuss or aa44.  Default is uuss.


       --dd _d_p_i Print resolution in dots per inch.


       --ff _c_f_g_f_i_l_e
              Read  parameters from the configuration file named _c_f_g_f_i_l_e.  See
              CONFIGURATION FILES ⟨#configfile⟩



       The offset adjustments you specify with --xx and --yy accumulate.  I.e.  if
       you specify them multiple times, the total offset adjustment is the sum
       of the adjustments you specify.  --xx 6600 --xx 112200 is the same as --xx 118800.

       The --vv option undoes any preceding --xx and --yy options, leaving the hori‐
       zontal and vertical adjustments their default values.



CCOONNFFIIGGUURRAATTIIOONN FFIILLEESS
       You  can use a configuration file to specify parameters rather than use
       invocation options.  ppbbmmttooppppaa processes the file //eettcc//ppbbmmttooppppaa..ccoonnff, if
       it  exists, before processing any options.  It then processes each con‐
       figuration file named by a --ff option in order, applying the  parameters
       from  the configuration file as if they were invocation options used in
       the place of the --ff option.

       Configuration files have the following format:

       ##_C_o_m_m_e_n_t
       _k_e_y_1 _v_a_l_u_e_1
       _k_e_y_2 _v_a_l_u_e_2
       [etc.]


       Valid _k_e_ys are vveerrssiioonn, xxooffffsseett, yyooffffsseett, ttooppmmaarrggiinn, lleeffttmmaarrggiinn, rriigghhtt‐‐
       mmaarrggiinn, bboottttoommmmaarrggiinn, ppaappeerrssiizzee, or any non-null prefix of these words.
       Valid values are the same as with the corresponding invocation  parame‐
       ters.


EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
       Print a test pattern:
       ppbbmmppaaggee || ppbbmmppppaa >>//ddeevv//llpp11


       Print three pages:
       ccaatt ppaaggee11..ppbbmm ppaaggee22..ppbbmm ppaaggee33..ppbbmm || ppbbmmppppaa >>//ddeevv//llpp11


       Print the Postscript file myfile.ps:
       gs -sDEVICE=rawpbm -q -dNOPAUSE -r600 \
          -sOutputFile=- myfile.ps ;\
       | pbmtoppa | lpr



CCAALLIIBBRRAATTIIOONN
       To  be  able  to print successfully and properly, you need to tell ppbbmm‐‐
       ttooppppaa an X and a Y offset appropriate for your printer to use when gen‐
       erating the page.  You can specify these offsets with the --xx and --yy in‐
       vocation options or with the xxooffff and yyooffff  parameters  in  a  ppbbmmttooppppaa
       configuration file.

       To determine the correct offsets, use the ppbbmmppaaggee program.

       If  while trying to do this calibration, the printer refuses to print a
       page, but just blinks all three lights, specify large margins (e.g. 600
       pixels -- one inch) via ppbbmmppaaggee invocation options while doing the cal‐
       ibration.

       For example:
       ppbbmmppaaggee || ppbbmmttooppppaa >>//ddeevv//llpp11

       or
       ppbbmmppaaggee || ppbbmmttooppppaa || llpprr --ll


       (if your printer filter recognizes the '-l' (direct output) parameter).

       In the test pattern, the grid is marked off in  pixel  coordinate  num‐
       bers.  Unfortunately, these coordinates are probably cut off before the
       edge of the paper.  You'll have to use a ruler to  estimate  the  pixel
       coordinate  of  the  left  and  top  edges of the actual sheet of paper
       (should be within +/- 300, may be negative; there are  600  pixels  per
       inch).

       Add these coordinates to the X and Y offsets by either editing the con‐
       figuration file or using the --xx  and --yy command-line parameters.

       When ppbbmmttooppppaa is properly calibrated, the center mark should be in  the
       center  of  the paper.  Also, the margins should be able to be as small
       as 1/4 inch without causing the printer to choke with 'blinking  lights
       syndrome'.


RREEDD HHAATT LLIINNUUXX IINNSSTTAALLLLAATTIIOONN
       RedHat  users  may  find the following tip from Panayotis Vryonis <_v_r_y_‐
       _p_a_n_@_h_o_l_._g_r> helpful.  The same should work for the 820 and 1000, but it
       hasn't been tested.  Also, use the pbmraw GSDriver if you have it; it's
       faster.

       Here is a tip to integrate HP720C capability in RedHat's printtool:

       Install pbmtoppa. Copy pbmtoppa to /usr/bin.

       Edit "printerdb" (in my system it is found  in  /usr/lib/rhs/rhs-print‐
       filters ) and append the following lines:


       ----------------------Cut here-----------------------

       StartEntry: DeskJet720C
         GSDriver: pbm
         Description: {HP DeskJet 720C}
         About: { \
               This driver drives the HP DeskJet 720C \
               inkjet printer. \
               It cannot do color printing. \
               IMPORTANT! Insert \
                  "- | pbm2ppa -" \
               in the "Extra GS Options " field.\
             }

         Resolution: {600} {600} {}

       EndEntry
       ----------------------------------------------------



       Now you can add an HP720C printer just like any other, using printtool.


